From: PS5_Proton <local>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: a kill waits for the fault handler to return on PS5

quit_handler ends a thread outside a syscall at once, and a thread in the trap or fault handler is outside one: the
handler runs on the signal stack beside the TEB, not the kernel stack is_inside_syscall measures. Both handlers take
the virtual mutex without masking (virtual_handle_fault, virtual_ps5_step_done), and a payload's execute fault
converts code to JIT memory under it, through the dmem engine's lock (patch 1127). A kill that lands mid-conversion
ended the thread with both locks held, and the process's exit then waited on them until the stall watch ended it.
Measured in gate-2026-10-16-browser-01 with PS5_EXEC_CLUSTER at 16 KiB, three cases of three: winedevice.exe
ended about 10 ms after its service thread 004c failed to load mountmgr.sys; the lock's owner was the first thread
NtTerminateProcess killed, 004c, which printed "thread 004c killed" (abort_thread) without ever releasing it, and
the engine still recorded the conversion's protect or release of a kernel32 code copy 7 s later, until the stall
watch ended the process. A thread marked inside a syscall returns through every frame first; this one did not.
Upstream has the same window, too short to see without conversions.
SIGQUIT joins the trap and fault handlers' mask, as ps5_mmu4k_enter already masks it for a run, so a kill lands
when the handler returns to the code it interrupted: user mode, where the thread ends with nothing held, or a
syscall, where it is marked. The suspend handler stays killable.
---
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
index a4becd6..92d5bc8 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
@@ -3795,6 +3795,11 @@ void signal_init_process(void)
     if (pw_sigaction( SIGQUIT, &sig_act, NULL ) == -1) goto error;
     sig_act.sa_sigaction = usr1_handler;
     if (pw_sigaction( SIGUSR1, &sig_act, NULL ) == -1) goto error;
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+    /* The trap and fault handlers take the virtual mutex and the dmem engine's lock on the signal stack, where
+     * quit_handler sees user mode and ends the thread at once with both held; a kill waits for the return instead. */
+    sigaddset( &sig_act.sa_mask, SIGQUIT );
+#endif
     sig_act.sa_sigaction = trap_handler;
     if (pw_sigaction( SIGTRAP, &sig_act, NULL ) == -1) goto error;
     sig_act.sa_sigaction = segv_handler;
